  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nModel uncertainty remains quite high for the first half of next week \nas the position of the high is likely to set up somewhere over \neastern New Mexico and western Texas by Monday. This position can \ncause higher uncertainty in moisture levels for our area with the \nlatest guidance trying to advect some moisture into our area by \nSunday or Monday, but NBM forecast PoPs are at most 10-15% for the \nlower deserts. At this point, we could continue to see mostly slight \nchances favoring the high terrain into early next week, but if \nmoisture does eventually improve enough storm chances are likely to \nbe higher than what the NBM is depicting. Another wildcard will be \nthe potential influence of an upper level trough that may attempt to \nmove into western portions of our region Sunday into Monday. If this \noccurs, it may be enough to improve convective potential across \nsoutheast California and western Arizona for Sunday and/or Monday. \nBy Tuesday, drier air will filter back into the region as the \nsubtropical high begins to retrograde back over our region. This \nwill end limit low end rain chances to the higher terrain areas with \nthe lower deserts remaining dry. By the latter half of next week, \nthe NBM shows PoPs being confined to the Mogollon Rim with no \nchances for our CWA. \n\nTemperatures are expected to continue to be more influenced by the \nstrong subtropical high as H5 heights hover between 593-596dm across \nthe Desert Southwest through early next week. Daytime highs are not \nexpected to change much from Sunday to Monday, barring any \nsignificant change with the convective chances. As mentioned above, \nby Tuesday the subtropical high will start to retrograde back to the \nwest and center will eventually settle over AZ/NM during the latter \nhalf of the workweek. The high will also strengthen to around 595-\n597dm during this time. The combination of the center settling over \nour region and strengthening will result in temperatures heating up \nto around 10 degrees above normal for this time of year. Afternoon \nhighs (morning lows) will be in the 110-116 (85-95) degree range \nacross the lower deserts and in the 100-110 (75-85) degree range \nacross the higher terrain areas. The increase in temperatures will \nalso result in an increase in HeatRisk, with widespread Extreme \nHeatRisk forecasted for the majority of the CWA for the latter half \nof the workweek. Due to these hot temperatures and widespread \nExtreme HeatRisk expected next week, the Extreme Heat Warning has \nbeen extended through next Friday.",
